{
  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `FeedbackForm` component includes good foundational accessibility practices: semantic labels via `htmlFor`, HTML5 validation attributes, native form elements, and high-contrast visible focus rings. However, serious design gaps undermine assistive technology and keyboard navigation during asynchronous submission:\n\n1. **Focus remains stranded or drops**: When the submit button becomes disabled (`disabled={status === 'submitting'}`), focus is evicted or stranded depending on browser implementation, and is never programmatically moved to the status message or restored upon success/error.\n2. **Vague, non-contextual success message**: \"Your submission was successful!\" does not specify *what* was submitted, whether records were updated, or provide follow-up actions (e.g., submitting another response, clearing data).\n3. **Double role announcement conflict**: Nesting an inner `<p role=\"alert\">` inside a container with `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"` creates conflicting announcement semantics across screen readers.\n4. **Form state retains values without clear intent**: The form values remain populated behind a generic success message without resetting or disabling the fields, confusing cognitive and screen reader users as to whether the form can or should be re-submitted.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore auditing the code in detail, the likely failure modes for an async feedback form were:\n- Live region announcement races or missed announcements due to timing/clearing tricks (`aria-atomic` / `setTimeout`).\n- Focus lost when the submit button is disabled while actively focused.\n- Form fields neither resetting nor disabling after successful submission, leading to ambiguity.\n- Success announcement being too generic or vague for screen reader users navigating out of context.\n- Live region semantics conflicting (`role=\"status\"` vs `role=\"alert\"`).\n\n*Audit outcome:* All of these predictions were confirmed in the implementation.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings\n\n#### 1. Focus loss / stranding during and after submission\n- **Location**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:119-125`\n  ```jsx\n  <button\n    type=\"submit\"\n    className=\"submit-button\"\n    disabled={status === 'submitting'}\n  >\n  ```\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order)\n- **Impacted Users**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: When the user activates the \"Send Feedback\" button, it immediately receives `disabled`. Browsers handle focus on currently-focused disabled buttons inconsistently: in Chrome/Firefox, focus either drops to `document.body` or becomes inert, breaking logical keyboard flow. Furthermore, after success or error, focus is never shifted to the status banner or back to an active interactive control. Keyboard users must Tab through the entire page from the beginning to reach the form again.\n- **Fix**: Either manage focus programmatically or use `aria-disabled=\"true\"` on the button instead of native `disabled` to keep focus stable without resetting the Tab order. On completion, programmatically shift focus to the status container via `tabIndex=\"-1\"` and `statusRef.current?.focus()`.\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings\n\n#### 2. Vague success message lacks context and follow-up guidance\n- **Location**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:62-64`\n  ```jsx\n  {status === 'success' && (\n    <p className=\"status-message success\">\n      Your submission was successful!\n    </p>\n  )}\n  ```\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), WCAG 3.3.4 (Error Prevention / Submission Confirmation)\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users, users with cognitive disabilities\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: A polite live region announcing only *\"Your submission was successful!\"* lacks essential context. A screen reader user operating in a complex page or dynamic workflow may not know what was submitted (e.g., feedback vs inquiry vs sign-up), whether an email confirmation was sent, or what to do next. Sighted and screen reader users alike see the form inputs still filled with their previous text below the banner, causing doubt as to whether the form is in an active, duplicate, or completed state.\n- **Fix**:\n  1. Make the message descriptive: *\"Your feedback has been sent. Thank you for helping us improve!\"*\n  2. Clear the form data upon success (or provide a clear \"Submit another response\" button).\n\n#### 3. Conflicting live region semantics (`role=\"alert\"` nested inside `role=\"status\"`)\n- **Location**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:48-69`\n  ```jsx\n  <div\n    ref={statusRef}\n    role=\"status\"\n    aria-live=\"polite\"\n    aria-atomic=\"true\"\n    aria-busy={status === 'submitting'}\n    className=\"form-status\"\n  >\n    ...\n    {status === 'error' && (\n      <p className=\"status-message error\" role=\"alert\">\n        {errorMessage}\n      </p>\n    )}\n  </div>\n  ```\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages)\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users (NVDA, VoiceOver, JAWS)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: The outer container declares `role=\"status\"` (which implies `aria-live=\"polite\"`). Nesting an inner element with `role=\"alert\"` (which implies `aria-live=\"assertive\"`) causes inconsistent announcement behavior across browser and screen reader combinations\u2014some will suppress the alert because the parent is polite, while others may emit doubled announcements.\n- **Fix**: Choose one region strategy. Either remove `role=\"alert\"` from the child and let the polite region announce the error naturally, or separate into distinct live regions: one polite for status/loading/success, and one dedicated assertive region for urgent errors.\n\n#### 4. Generic error message lacks actionable recovery advice\n- **Location**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:38`\n  ```javascript\n  setErrorMessage('Something went wrong. Please try again.');\n  ```\n- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.3 (Error Suggestion)\n- **Impacted Users**: Cognitive disabilities, all users experiencing network failures\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**: \"Something went wrong. Please try again\" does not explain whether the network failed, the server crashed, or data was malformed, nor does it tell the user whether their data was preserved.\n- **Fix**: Provide descriptive, actionable error guidance: *\"We were unable to send your feedback due to a connection error. Your entered information is preserved\u2014please check your connection and try sending again.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings\n\n#### 1. Input fields lack error association (`aria-invalid`, `aria-describedby`)\n- **Location**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:73-116`\n- **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WCAG 3.3.1 (Error Identification)\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users\n- **Confidence**: MEDIUM\n- **Why this matters**: When the form enters the `error` state, the inputs are not marked with `aria-invalid=\"true\"`, nor are they tied to the error message via `aria-describedby=\"status-message-id\"`. When the user navigates through fields to retry, AT announces no association with the error state.\n- **Fix**: Associate fields with the status container ID using `aria-describedby` when `status === 'error'`.\n\n#### 2. Artificial 200ms delay hack for live region clearing\n- **Location**: `FeedbackForm.jsx:32-35`\n  ```javascript\n  setStatus('clearing');\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    setStatus('success');\n  }, 200);\n  ```\n- **WCAG Citation**: WAI-ARIA Live Region Practices\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users\n- **Confidence**: MEDIUM\n- **Why this matters**: Unmounting and remounting content in 200ms using an undocumented `clearing` state is a fragile workaround to trigger screen reader mutation observers. In slower mobile devices or virtual screen readers, rapid unmounting can cause the region to stay silent or drop text.\n- **Fix**: Maintain a persistent status element and update its text content directly.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements\n\n- **Explicit required indicator**: Add visible `*` or `(required)` text inside the `<label>` elements so sighted and cognitive users do not have to guess if all fields are compulsory before triggering native validation.\n- **Field preservation indication**: Explicitly indicate to the user after success if the form was reset, or provide an explicit action button: `Submit another feedback entry`.\n\n---\n\n## Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Nested conflicting live roles (`status` + `alert`), vague success announcement, potential focus drop on disabled button. |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Button becomes `disabled` while focused; no focus management to success/error summary. |\n| **Low vision** | **LOW** | Clear 3px focus indicators, solid text contrast, standard layout. |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | Form retains old text after generic \"success\" banner; generic \"something went wrong\" error lacks guidance. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | Spinner animation is minor and localized. (Can optionally add `@media (prefers-reduced-motion)` for the spinner). |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio or video content involved. |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **LOW** | Color palette passes standard contrast thresholds. |\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user**: Hearing only *\"Your submission was successful!\"* without prior notification of focus movement or what happened to the form leaves uncertainty. If the error occurs, conflicting `status`/`alert` roles risk silence in JAWS and doubled speech in VoiceOver.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Pressing `Space` or `Enter` on the submit button disables it. Depending on the browser, focus either drops to `body` or sticks on an un-focusable node. When submission succeeds, focus is not on the confirmation message, requiring extraneous Tab presses.\n- **Low vision user**: High visibility focus rings are present. However, if the viewport is zoomed to 200%, the status container above the form might be scrolled out of view if focus is lost and doesn't scroll into the viewport.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: If a user submits feedback and sees the same text in the inputs with a one-sentence message above it, they may repeatedly click submit wondering if it actually went through.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REVISE**: While native form semantics and styling are cleanly written, the asynchronous flow suffers from keyboard focus dropping/stranding, ambiguous completion feedback, and fragile live region markup. These issues prevent the component from delivering a predictable, robust assistive experience under real-world usage.\n\n### Upgrade Requirements:\n1. Prevent focus dropping on submit: use `aria-disabled=\"true\"` or programmatically focus the status container when the request resolves.\n2. Contextualize the success message and clear the form data (or offer a \"Send another\" flow).\n3. Normalize the live region hierarchy by removing nested conflicting live roles (`role=\"alert\"` inside `role=\"status\"`).\n4. Provide meaningful recovery details in error state announcements.\n",
